Q: VS Code Treeview gets information about the node in focus A: <p>&quot;Does the VS Code API provide a hook similar to onDidChangeFocus to capture keyboard-driven movement?&quot;</p>
<p>No, but first I thought there is another way - intercept the command <code>list.focusDown</code> which is what is triggered when you mouse down your tree.  Something like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>commands.registerCommand( 'list.focusDown', async ( treeItem ) =&gt; {
    console.log( treeItem );   // undefined

    // change tree selection, this will fire onDidChangeSelection()
    await symbolView.reveal( symbolView.selection[ 0 ].children[ 0 ] as any, {
        select: true,
        focus: true,
        expand: true
    } );
} ),
</code></pre>
<p>This does work but there are 2 problems.</p>
<p>First, the command doesn't return an argument - <code>treeItem</code> is always undefined.  This is solvable since you know the tree selection is the previous item and it could be looked up.</p>
<p>Second, <code>list.focusDown</code> is used in many places in the vscode UI and you want to trigger it only when the tree has focus.  But that is not easy to know and not worth trying to track.  And there will never be a <code>getContext()</code> api in vscode apparently...</p>
<p>So I thought you should be able to create a keybinding in your <code>package.json</code> that could make use of context values.  Try this in <code>package.json</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>&quot;commands&quot;: [
  {
    &quot;command&quot;: &quot;symbolsTree.focusDown&quot;,
    &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Mouse down in the SymbolsTree&quot;,
    &quot;enablement&quot;: &quot;focusedView == 'symbolsTree' &amp;&amp; listFocus&quot;
  }
]
// symbolsTree is the id of my TreeView
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>&quot;keybindings&quot;: [
  {
    &quot;key&quot;: &quot;down&quot;,
    &quot;command&quot;: &quot;symbolsTree.focusDown&quot;,
    &quot;when&quot;: &quot;focusedView == 'symbolsTree' &amp;&amp; listFocus&quot;
  }
</code></pre>
<p>and in your code:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>commands.registerCommand( 'symbolsTree.focusDown', async ( treeItem: any ) =&gt; {
    console.log( treeItem );  // still undefined so of no use

    // change tree selection, this will fire your onDidChangeSelection()
    await symbolView.reveal( symbolView.selection[ 0 ].children[ 0 ] as any, {
        select: true,
        focus: true,
        expand: true
    } );
} )
</code></pre>
<p>This seems to work quite nicely.  You will have to handle mouse <code>up</code> in a similar fashion and perhaps other keys like pageDown/pageUp.</p>
<p>And you need a way to figure out what is the next TreeItem that you moused to.  In the code above I just hardcoded the first child but you will need to replace that.</p>
<p>AI suggested that you create a flat array (if your TreeItems have children) of your TreeItems and their children - walking the TreeItems and pushing them into an array.  And then you can find the selected TreeItem from Treeview.selection and get the next array item.</p>
<p>That shouldn't be too hard to implement but you need to consider how you will handle collapsed TreeItems (if any) and update the flattened array whenever there is any change to a TreeItem.</p>
<hr />
<p>This would all be easy to do if this <a href="https://github.com/EhabY/vscode/blob/d23158246aaa474996f2237f735461ad47e41403/src/vscode-dts/vscode.proposed.treeViewActiveItem.d.ts#L10-L29" rel="nofollow noreferrer">vscode.proposed.treeViewActiveItem</a> is ever added.  It would directly expose the focussed item (as opposed to the selected item).</p>
<p>If you are interested in that functionality you should add your use case to <a href="https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/issues/185563" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Async data tree doesn't maintain consistency on focus and selection events</a> which must be resolved before the proposed api can be merged.</p>
